.TH NIX-SEAL 1 "2026-07-31" "nix-seal 0.1.0-alpha.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nix-seal \- security-first, offline-first secret management for Nix
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nix-seal
[\fB--json\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nix-seal
manages standard age ciphertext for NixOS, nix-darwin, and Home Manager. Public
policy is compiled deterministically; plaintext is accepted only from standard
input or an explicit private editor transaction and is activated only in a
restricted runtime generation.
.PP
The default delivery mode is administrator-to-target rekeying with signed target
artifacts. Direct target-recipient delivery is advanced and retains the risk
that a stolen target identity can decrypt historical ciphertext addressed to it.
.SH SAFETY
Secret values are never accepted through command-line arguments or ordinary
environment variables. JSON output never contains plaintext. The explicitly
named \fBsecret reveal\fR operation is the sole command that writes plaintext
to standard output.
.PP
The \fBsecret create\fR, \fBsecret import\fR, \fBsecret edit\fR, and
\fBrotate\fR commands accept \fB--format json\fR, \fBtoml\fR, \fByaml\fR, or
\fBdotenv\fR to validate a bounded logical collection before it is encrypted.
The original bytes are retained. dotenv validation accepts only unique
shell-compatible \fBKEY=VALUE\fR entries and does not evaluate shell syntax.
.PP
Generator prompts are non-interactive by default and must be supplied through
owner-only \fB--prompt-file ID=PATH\fR inputs. The explicit
\fBgenerate --interactive\fR mode reads missing prompts only from a verified
controlling \fB/dev/tty\fR, masks hidden prompts with terminal settings restored
on failure, bounds responses to 1 MiB, and never places prompt values in argv,
the environment, the plan, or logs. Single-line prompts finish with Enter;
multiline prompts finish with Ctrl-D.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Create an empty valid public plan without creating keys or secrets.
.TP
.B plan, check, doctor
Compile, inspect, validate, and diagnose public policy.
.TP
.B key, identity, group
Manage identities and public policy metadata.
The optional \fBkey generate --passphrase\fR mode writes a standard age
scrypt-encrypted human recovery identity after protected terminal prompts;
passphrases are never read from argv, stdin, or the environment.
.TP
.B secret, rotate
Create, import, edit, reveal, inspect, delete, recipient-rekey, and rotate
canonical secrets. Recipient rekey is dry-run by default and requires an
explicit \fB--yes\fR for atomic replacement; it does not rotate the value.
.TP
.B rekey, artifact, provision, cache
Create, verify, exchange, and garbage-collect signed ciphertext-only artifacts.
.TP
.B generate, template
Run constrained declared generators and validate public templates.
.TP
.B migrate
Perform dry-run-first migration inspection or explicit stream re-encryption.
.TP
.B schema, completions
Print versioned JSON Schemas and shell completions.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/nix-seal/v1
Ciphertext-only rekey cache.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit code 0 indicates success. Code 2 is command-line usage. Codes 3 through 6
respectively identify policy, cryptographic/approval, cache/storage, and runtime
activation failures.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR age (1)
.PP
Project documentation: https://github.com/IanHollow/nix-seal
